# services/pipeline/.env — copied from this file by `pnpm dev` / `mise install`
# when missing (scripts/dev/bootstrap-env.mjs). See the README.
#
# One file for the whole service: the queue system below, and the workflows it
# runs further down. `services/atproto-cron-sync/.env` used to hold the second
# half; that package is gone and its variables moved here verbatim.

# --- the queue system ------------------------------------------------------

# The local dev Redis, from the repo's docker-compose.yml. BullMQ stores every
# queue, job and scheduler here; wiping it (`docker compose down -v`) resets the
# workflows to empty.
REDIS_URL=redis://:butterydev@localhost:56379

# Where the Fastify server (and the Bull Board UI at /ui) listens.
PORT=3002

# Basic-auth credentials for the board and the job API. Leave the password blank
# locally — the dev stack is loopback-only and an unset password means no login
# prompt. It is REQUIRED in production: the board shows every job payload and
# lets a visitor retry, promote and delete jobs.
PIPELINE_AUTH_USER=buttery
PIPELINE_AUTH_PASSWORD=

# Jobs one worker process interleaves. Replicas are the other dial — see the
# README's autoscaling section for which one to reach for.
# PIPELINE_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# Autoscaling is Railway-only and opt-in: without RAILWAY_API_TOKEN the loop
# never starts, which is why nothing here configures it. See .railway/railway.ts.

# --- workflow: atproto-sync ------------------------------------------------

# Cron pattern (UTC) for the atproto → Postgres sweep. Deliberately BLANK
# locally: a laptop should not quietly sweep the live atmosphere in the
# background. Railway sets it to hourly (see .railway/railway.ts).
#
# Uncomment for a sweep every five minutes and watch it in the board, or trigger
# one on demand at any time without a schedule:
#   curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3002/jobs/atproto-sync
# ATPROTO_SYNC_SCHEDULE=*/5 * * * *

# How many repos one sweep may have in flight at once, across every worker
# replica. The sweep fans out every repo it found and the queue holds them; this
# is what decides how many actually run. Enforced in Redis by BullMQ, so it holds
# however many replicas the autoscaler has running.
# ATPROTO_SYNC_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=8

# The local dev Postgres, from the repo's docker-compose.yml. Where the sweep
# writes; the schema itself belongs to @buttery/web's migrations.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:butterydev@localhost:55432/buttery

# Which network a sweep reads — the same answer whether it runs on the schedule,
# from the board, or from `pnpm --filter @buttery/pipeline sync:once`. These
# defaults are the real atmosphere; swap in the commented values to sweep the
# local atproto dev-env instead (its PDS list replaces relay enumeration —
# dev-env has no relay).
ATPROTO_PLC_URL=https://plc.directory        # dev-env: http://localhost:2582
RELAY_URL=https://relay1.us-east.bsky.network
SYNC_PDS_URL=                                # dev-env: http://localhost:2583

# Optional narrowing; either makes the sweep partial (no delete reconciliation).
# SYNC_MAX_REPOS=25
# SYNC_ONLY_DID=did:plc:…
# SYNC_CONCURRENCY=8

# --- workflow: recipe-enrichment -------------------------------------------

# How many recipes the fleet may classify at once, across every worker replica.
# The producer never throttles — both write paths and the backfill fan out
# everything and let the queue hold it — so this is the only thing standing
# between an atproto-sync sweep of thousands of repos and a fleet doing nothing
# else. Enforced in Redis by BullMQ, so it survives the autoscaler moving the
# replica count around underneath it. Read by the SERVER, which reconciles it
# onto the queue at boot the same way it does ATPROTO_SYNC_MAX_IN_FLIGHT.
#
# There is deliberately no schedule variable here: backfill is a manual act
# (plan D15), reached with
#   curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3002/jobs/recipe-enrichment \
#     -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"backfill","data":{"limit":50}}'
# RECIPE_ENRICHMENT_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=16

# --- workflow: recipe-enrichment, the LLM second opinion --------------------

# `llm-enrich` runs after every successful rules classification and asks a model
# to read the lines the lexicon missed, judge the dimensions no rule covers
# (cuisine, meal type, spice level, the macro/paleo diets), and give a second
# opinion on the rest. It is FAIL-CLOSED: with no override and no PostHog, it
# marks the recipe `skipped` and calls nothing. See
# services/pipeline/src/workflows/recipe-enrichment/llm/.

# The override, and the ONLY way to run the LLM locally (there is no PostHog in
# dev, and no PostHog means no LLM call). "false" hard-disables; "true" bypasses
# the flag entirely; UNSET defers to the PostHog flag `llm-enrichment-enabled`,
# which in production starts at 0%. Checked BEFORE the flag on purpose —
# evaluating a flag captures a $feature_flag_called event per evaluation.
# LLM_ENRICHMENT_ENABLED=true

# Which entry in lib/ai/provider.ts's registry runs. Only `openrouter` ships
# today — it is a gateway, so a different MODEL is an LLM_ENRICHMENT_MODEL edit
# here rather than a new registry entry. A direct-to-vendor entry is what the
# registry is still for (llm plan §6.1).
LLM_ENRICHMENT_PROVIDER=openrouter

# The model id: an OpenRouter routing slug (`vendor/model`), with deliberately
# NO default in code, so a missing or wrong id is a loud error someone reads at
# deploy rather than a constant to maintain. Browse openrouter.ai/models.
#
# Chosen by measurement, not vibes: `test/llm-enrichment/MODEL-RUNS.md` in
# @buttery/food runs the fixture set 3x through each candidate and scores how the
# DECISIONS diverge. Before swapping this value, re-run that set — the failure
# that matters is not "worse prose", it is an allergen verdict moving in the
# less-cautious direction, and only the fixtures surface it.
LLM_ENRICHMENT_MODEL=google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite

# Blank locally: without a key the provider refuses to build and the step
# records an error instead of guessing. Never commit a real one.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=             # default https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# --- PostHog (the flag, the prompt, and $ai_generation capture) -------------

# Same allowlist semantics as the web service: an explicit runtime opt-in, so
# unset / empty / misspelled all resolve to OFF. Blank locally on purpose — a
# laptop should not write into the production analytics project, and the
# LLM_ENRICHMENT_ENABLED override above is what lets you run the step anyway.
POSTHOG_ENABLED=

# Event capture: a public `phc_` project token against the INGESTION host.
POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=
POSTHOG_HOST=                    # default https://us.i.posthog.com

# Prompt Management is the odd one out (llm plan §5.2): @posthog/ai's `Prompts`
# client authenticates with a PERSONAL api key (scope `llm_prompt:read`) against
# the APP host (https://us.posthog.com), and uses POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN above to
# select the project. Both are needed; neither alone is enough.
#
# Absent either, llm/prompt-fetch.ts falls back to the prompt committed in
# llm/prompt.ts and records llm_prompt_version = null, so "which recipes ran on
# the fallback" stays a query rather than a mystery.
#
# There is no POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID here any more: it existed for a hand-rolled
# REST call that the official client replaced, and nothing reads it. The id
# (538428) is still what a human needs for the §5 PostHog-side setup.
POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=

# Only needed if PostHog cannot price the model from $ai_model + token counts
# (llm plan §5.3): set both and the generation span carries
# $ai_input_token_price / $ai_output_token_price so PostHog can compute cost.
#
# UNVERIFIED since generations moved to the OpenTelemetry path: whether
# PostHog's OTLP ingestion honours these attributes the way posthog-node's
# capture did is not something this repo can establish without live project
# access, and PostHog's docs truncate before the attribute-mapping section.
# They are still emitted because an ignored attribute costs nothing and
# produces ABSENT cost rather than wrong cost. Check the first real
# generations before trusting a cost number that depends on them.
LLM_INPUT_TOKEN_PRICE_USD=
LLM_OUTPUT_TOKEN_PRICE_USD=

# --- AI observability (OpenTelemetry) ---------------------------------------

# Nothing new to set. Generations now reach PostHog as OpenTelemetry spans
# rather than as hand-built $ai_generation events, and the span processor
# (plugins/telemetry.ts) reads the SAME two variables the capture client does:
# POSTHOG_ENABLED must be "true" and POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN must be set, or no
# tracer provider is registered at all — no exporter, no background timer, no
# cost to a dev machine for the module existing.
#
# POSTHOG_HOST is the ingestion host for both paths. The personal key above is
# for Prompt Management only and plays no part in telemetry.
#
# Why OTel and not @posthog/ai's model wrapper: withTracing throws for any
# model whose specificationVersion is not v2/v3, and @openrouter/ai-sdk-provider
# under ai@7 reports v4. Its own error message names this as the replacement.
# See services/pipeline/src/plugins/telemetry.ts.
